{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt","id":1132593,"title":"Specialization doubt","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-22T07:03:43-07:00","id":1132593,"title":"Specialization doubt","content":"So, some of my player are picking their specializations in a few fields, but some of their requests sounds a bit too convenient to me.<br /> <br /> i.e.: brawl specialization: claw attacks. Stealth specialization: hiding (from sight).<br /> Aren't these fields too broad and common? I mean, doubling 10s on every claw attack seems a LOT to me, especially when the suggested specs on the manual are mostly combat styles unusable in Crinos form. But bites and claw attacks should be, by logic, legit specializations for &quot;brawl&quot;. Same thing for hiding: sure, you won't double your 10s on sneaking or shadowing, but cmon, 60% of rolls include sight.<br /> <br /> Would you allow them? Do you have suggestions on how handle or specialize that kind of natural attack?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-22T07:15:19-07:00","id":1132598,"title":"","content":"I haven't reviewed the list of specialties in all the *Splat*20's but given that Tooth &amp; Claw used to be a legit fighting style in the game, saying just Claw Attacks seems like the player should be kicking themselves for giving up the extra rolls on their bites (which are a lower difficulty).<br /> <br /> Hiding from Sight sounds like Shadowing to me and I would have my player take that. Given that you are more likely to get caught by a Garou's sense of smell, hearing, or by some sixth sense gift, I don't see that it actually gives them that much greater of an advantage in stating just Sight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1619-derzhuzad","name":"Derzhuzad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-22T11:33:15-07:00","id":1132721,"title":"","content":"I would consider those specializations too broad. Situations for specialties should be infrequent in most games, or if they occur more represent an actual, legitimate field of focus (like taking the specialty Brainy Surgery if you have Medicine 5, and then having your day job be a brain surgeon).<br /> <br /> A Brawl specialty in Claws or a Stealth specialty in Hiding likely covers 50% or more of most uses of that ability in most chronicles. Specialties are not meant to apply that many times. This is almost as blatant as having a Brawl specialty in &quot;Fighting&quot; and a Stealth specialty in 'Undetected&quot;.<br /> <br /> Using the W20 examples, the listed examples of Brawl specialties are all a particular style of fighting. They'd mostly apply in formal competitive settings (not real combat, but sports), but I could see situations where they'd apply in real combat sporadically (Boxing could mean a brutal punch of Bashing damage in Homid form; Wrestling to restrain someone, Martial Arts to throwing someone).<br /> <br /> The Stealth specialties are all specific situations when the specialty would apply (only when Shadowing someone, only in the Urban environment, only when Taking Point ahead of your pack, only when hiding in Crowds, only when Hiding Objects).<br /> <br /> So for Brawl, I'd let them pick one competitive sport (and discuss what real combat situations it would also apply in), or one of the lesser used non-damage dealing maneuvers or high difficulty damage maneuvers (disarming someone, blinding someone (which is DC 9), sweeping, or jaw lock).<br /> <br /> For Stealth, I'd ask them what specific situations would the specialty apply. &quot;When I hide&quot; is not a specific situation. It might be a specific terrain (urban, woodland, prairie, swamps - and there may be certain terrain not allowed as it provides too few opportunities). Or a specific circumstance (when I have a prepared ambush spot, when I am shadowing someone and following them, when I try to lose myself in a crowd). Or certain kinds of tasks (I am smuggling goods into the country past customs, when I want to hide objects so they aren't taken by the enemy, when I carry information out in a spy mission).<br /> <br /> Specialties are as much as a roleplay thing as they are a mechanic advantage. A specialty should be something that distinguishes the character as a true expert in a focused field. It is something they are devoting a lot of time and effort to master, an actual subset within that discipline. When something is way too broad the ST is definitely empowered to say that is not a legitimate specialty.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-22T12:06:04-07:00","id":1132735,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132721#post1132721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Using the W20 examples, the listed examples of Brawl specialties are all a particular style of fighting. They'd mostly apply in formal competitive settings (not real combat, but sports), but I could see situations where they'd apply in real combat sporadically (Boxing could mean a brutal punch of Bashing damage in Homid form; Wrestling to restrain someone, Martial Arts to throwing someone).<br /> <br /> <br /> So for Brawl, I'd let them pick one competitive sport (and discuss what real combat situations it would also apply in), or one of the lesser used non-damage dealing maneuvers or high difficulty damage maneuvers (disarming someone, blinding someone (which is DC 9), sweeping, or jaw lock).<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>I've been on various martial arts for years and I have to disagree with this part. The examples given sure have a sport component to them, but honestly someone who knows how to box for example has a rather large advantage over someone who doesnt have any formal training. If you are looking for self defense and not sports, if you find a good instructor (and some of your own thought) you'd be able to use most of these styles in real combat. A good martial artist sticks to their strengths. I don't see how there should be much of a talk on (If I specialize in Karate for example, and I decide to just stick to moves that are in the art then the specialization comes up). <br /> I also don't feel the ones listed are the limit. A lot of mainstream arts (karate, Muay Thai, Krav Maga) are multifaceted. Krav is an amalgamation of other styles and modern Muay Thai has been influenced by boxing. This would give them both great punching power and leg work as well. Krav and Karate also come with a variety of grappling to it. Brawl is meant to be unarmed combat, which for a human is hands and and feet, but for garou that DOES include claws and teeth. I get &quot;claw&quot; or bite may come off as extreme, but otherwise how would Red Talon Ahrouns specialize (I don't see them learning Tae kwon do, and sadly the backstory and culture to kalindo is too restricted)? Thats where role play comes in i suppose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","user":"CeltSPZ","date":". 08-22-2017, 03:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-22T12:46:46-07:00","id":1132749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132735#post1132735\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The examples given sure have a sport component to them, but honestly someone who knows how to box for example has a rather large advantage over someone who doesnt.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course they do which is why they are skilled at Brawl to begin with. My comment more had to do with the nature of the specialization.<br /> <br /> Some who is a trained boxer, or any other martial artist, knows how to fight. However, if you took one guy and told him, purely box (or use karate or tae kwon do or whatever), and another guy equally skilled and told them to do WHATEVER is necessary to win. Then the second guy beats the first guy because the first guy is restricted. Which is why formalized martial arts/boxing whatever usually doesn't work in a real fight/MMA atmosphere.<br /> <br /> But of course you can use what you learn when training to fight outside the ring.<br /> <br /> But when it comes to the Specialty, I am assuming here that the specialty is being used in these formal kinds of matches. A trained boxer is taught how to win the formalized ritual of boxing which has rules that don't apply in real life. In a martial arts competition, you can't do just anything. Some moves are illegal. A boxer knows he can use the clock to his advantage which you can't do in a real fight. Or that he doesn't need to worry about someone hitting him below the belt. That affects how he fights in a boxing match. That is where you get the mechanical bonus.<br /> <br /> Of course, that is too restrictive as generally players would never or rarely be in a situation where they'd fight in those formal matches. That's why I'd also say the ST should identify specific moves in real combat where they'd get the bonus as well as they could apply their training to that. A boxer still knows how to punch really good and that will cross over.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132735#post1132735\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Brawl is meant to be unarmed combat, which for a human is hands and and feet, but for garou that DOES include claws and teeth. I get &quot;claw&quot; or bite may come off as extreme, but otherwise how would Red Talon Ahrouns specialize (I don't see them learning Tae kwon do, and sadly the backstory and culture to kalindo is too restricted)? Thats where role play comes in i suppose.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why I also included the option to have a specialty in certain kind of maneuvers like sweeping, or jaw lock). I imagine a lupus who took a specialty in Brawl would pick a style he'd normally use as a wolf. There are Brawl manuevers which lupus can do like Jaw Lock, Hamstring. Or perhaps a maneuver only usable in pack tactics like Fur Gnarl.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-22T13:12:48-07:00","id":1132763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132749#post1132749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> But when it comes to the Specialty, I am assuming here that the specialty is being used in these formal kinds of matches. A trained boxer is taught how to win the formalized ritual of boxing which has rules that don't apply in real life. In a martial arts competition, you can't do just anything. Some moves are illegal. A boxer knows he can use the clock to his advantage which you can't do in a real fight. Or that he doesn't need to worry about someone hitting him below the belt. That affects how he fights in a boxing match. That is where you get the mechanical bonus.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>What about styles that aren't intended for sport then? Would you disallow Krav Maga, or Jeet Kune Do for example? The styles where the emphasis is on self defense/combat and not on timed bouts and where you are free to do what you want? In my own experience the schools I've gone to for Krav, JKD, Muay Thai, and Karate haven't put the emphasis on competition but instead on real life practical uses. I just feel assuming its formal is a bit of a stretch since the descriptor to brawl doesn't sound at all like its limited to that. This also is if we are going only by W20 books (since the Book of Combat from 1st ed made it clear you could go for any style....I've really wanted a 20th edition to that book since already so much has changed and well...that book...showed a real lack of understanding in many of the styles that it covered.)<br /> <br /> Side Note: I'm asking fore genuine curiosity about your opinions. I know tone can't be picked up over the net, so I don't want you to think I'm some screaming TAPOUT wearing cliche here.<br /> <br /> Edit: For what it's worth I did notice that W20 Core states &quot;weaponless martial art&quot; and next to the list it does say &quot;ect.&quot;. The &quot;ect.&quot; from how I read it would mean styles not intdended for sport but for actual combat would be included but they only have so much space on that list and are merely listing some of the more common styles (as far as the U.S. is concerned).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","user":"CeltSPZ","date":". 08-22-2017, 05:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-22T14:28:47-07:00","id":1132805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Derzhuzad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132598#post1132598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't reviewed the list of specialties in all the *Splat*20's but given that Tooth &amp; Claw used to be a legit fighting style in the game, saying just Claw Attacks seems like the player should be kicking themselves for giving up the extra rolls on their bites (which are a lower difficulty).<br /> <br /> Hiding from Sight sounds like Shadowing to me and I would have my player take that. Given that you are more likely to get caught by a Garou's sense of smell, hearing, or by some sixth sense gift, I don't see that it actually gives them that much greater of an advantage in stating just Sight.</div> </div> </div> </div>i on the same boat, specialization claws is useful but no the be-all-end-all of combat.<br /> <br /> Challenges with any kind of weapon, the need of not using the Crinos for example. The specialization is only useful with Crinos, hispo and maybe dalu (or homid with partial change)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-23T12:32:12-07:00","id":1133115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132763#post1132763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What about styles that aren't intended for sport then? Would you disallow Krav Maga, or Jeet Kune Do for example? The styles where the emphasis is on self defense/combat and not on timed bouts and where you are free to do what you want?</div> </div> </div> </div>It sounds like you are saying then that this isn't actually a specialty (since there is no focus), it is just really good Brawl. So being trained in that system is just justification for having really good Brawl skills. Of course, I would allow the player to pick a specialization based on their training just like I would any other person with 4 or 5 dots of Brawl. But the Specialty wouldn't be called Krav Maga or Jeet Kune Do since it is too vague. I'd allow Grappling or Sweeping along the guidelines of what I earlier said about non-damage maneuvers or high difficulty maneuvers.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-23T13:08:15-07:00","id":1133129,"title":"","content":"Alright, granted I'd disagree. Krav and JKD are rather focused, they are just wider then boxing or wrestling. The book lists karate for example which itself has kicks, punches as well as grappling, not unlike the two styles I listed (i can't speak for TKD or judo as I have not trained in either, though I did train in a style of karate and it wasn't focused on sport). The book pulled a grab bag of common styles with Karate among them (and Karate is just term for Japanese martial arts with many styles listed within such as Gojo Ryu or Isshin Ryu), then it lists &quot;ect.&quot;, which means you aren't limited by these at all. I don't see how &quot;brawl&quot; was meant to assume only the sporting angle of fighting and not serious combat.<br /> <br /> To reach a 4 or 5 you're near or at the top of human potential. To me that says you have mastered your specialty, probably beyond a black belt. So as a result you get your boost with you're style. Some styles are just more versatile then others. Personally I think the real problem is that it's not that much do to reach a 4 or 5 in abilities, no hard rules on when a person should be able to reach it (to cut down on pc vs gm arguments over the time table), and you can stack up a boat load of 5s without having to worry about the time in a day to keep all your abilities at that level (i.e. practice for brawl then melee then fire arms for example if someone wanted to go uber combat) while still having time to do anything else (moots, fight the when, kin relations, pack bonding ect.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-24T13:13:24-07:00","id":1133525,"title":"","content":"It seems to me you want to turn this into a discussion of which martial art is better in real life and not how specialties should work mechanically.<br /> <br /> Let me turn this back onto you. Under the way you run it, when DON'T you get use of your specialty?<br /> <br /> Because if I understand you rightly, there are very little situations where you don't get the benefit of your specialty when fighting. The player can always say he is using his fighting style. A specialty is not something that should be used near 100% of the time. It should only be used when certain conditions are meant.<br /> <br /> Or if you as an ST are saying certain styles are mechanically better than others, then you're essentially doing the same thing by pushing the players to one specialty. &quot;If you take Krav Maga, you always get to use it in combat. But if you take any other martial art, which I consider inferior, then you'll only get it some of the time.&quot; You've just told your PCs that the only useful Brawl specialty in your game is Krav Maga. And your NPCs should be mostly built like that to if they are serious about combat.<br /> <br /> Either of those are pretty bad STing.<br /> <br /> By the way, you keep saying my version of Brawl specialties cannot be used in real combat. You are wrong. In each of my posts, I mention that there should be a maneuver they can use that is useful in combat even if they take a &quot;style&quot; specialty as long as it is something associated with that style. That makes it equivalent to a non-style specialty. I can understand someone overlooking a point in one post. But when they do it on every post, it begins to look like they are doing it on purpose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-24T15:57:34-07:00","id":1133571,"title":"","content":"First, no I wasn't turning it into which martial art style is better in real life. I was pointing out that there are styles that include more moves then others, and not all of them have a sport element to them. I don't feel those styles should be watered down just because they involve more then just punching or just grappling. Muay Thai is more diverse then boxing, I don't think someone who takes it should be stuck with &quot;only kicks&quot; or &quot;only knees&quot; just because boxing only has punching, and wrestling only has grappling.<br /> <br /> I did disagree with you saying that Krav Maga and JKD wouldn't count as specialties, as they have &quot;no focus&quot;. &quot;Karate&quot; is listed which is a catch all term for a series of unarmed Japanese combat styles, and all the ones I'm aware of would be called as unfocused as Krav or JKD under your description.<br /> <br /> Also I disagree that its &quot;bad STing&quot;. I allow people who take a more diverse martial art style a wider list of uses. Some styles are limited while others are not. I personally see no reason to try to make all styles limited to a set number of moves, because in reality they aren't. Also my NPC's aren't built to all take the more diverse styles either. As an ST I'm not worried about wowing my players in combat, I build them to fill certain story requirements. Many times they don't have brawl specialties at all because I can't think of one that fits the NPC (this is assuming they have a 4 to begin with). I rarely optimize my NPC's, but if my PC's want to be that way, so long as it fits their character I'm ok with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1132749#post1132749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But when it comes to the Specialty, I am assuming here that the specialty is being used in these formal kinds of matches.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Lastly this is what I'm referencing when I talk about your view of brawl. Its that I disagree with your assumption that the specialization is geared around formal matches. I am not disagreeing that your version cannot be used in real combat. I'm talking about the fact that there are styles out there not intended for sport. There are people who train in styles with no intention of using them in the ring, but instead for self-defense. The book gives no evidence that those styles, or non-sport related aspects of the arts should be excluded.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","user":"CeltSPZ","date":". 08-24-2017, 11:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-24T17:44:51-07:00","id":1133606,"title":"","content":"And as I think on it, I do have a question about how game breaking would it really be? Most of the more involved moves (joint locks, kicks, ect.) have higher diffs. If you were to have a boxer, and they stuck to only punching with a lower base diff, wouldn't his specialty pay off more often then then someone kicking or throwing? Since successes to hit also add into damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-25T11:35:52-07:00","id":1133889,"title":"","content":"The problem with a lot of martial art styles is that people practice them in a too controlled environment, then when you get thrown into all the chaos, adrenaline and violence of an actual street fight it all goes to shit. A particularly example of this I often see is the way people suggest you disarm a knife wielder, most of the ways suggested are ridicules and you'll get your fingers sliced off trying, fights happen FAST and they are never clean.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13729-the-direful-spring","name":"The Direful Spring","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-25T16:26:13-07:00","id":1133983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Direful Spring</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1133889#post1133889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with a lot of martial art styles is that people practice them in a too controlled environment, then when you get thrown into all the chaos, adrenaline and violence of an actual street fight it all goes to shit. A particularly example of this I often see is the way people suggest you disarm a knife wielder, most of the ways suggested are ridicules and you'll get your fingers sliced off trying, fights happen FAST and they are never clean.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well yes, chaos and adrenaline mess up all your plans and practice. The goal is enough practice and muscle memory will HOPEFULLY kick in. Though you are right, some places that teach things that aren't that practical. Anytime weapons are involved like a knife, things suck. We joke where I train that the winner of a knife fight is the guy who dies in the ambulance instead of at the scene. <br /> <br /> As for the game, this is where I think higher diffs would reflect some things just being harder. Its easier to throw a punch then it is to isolate a arm with a knife and then disarm the attacker. Granted if you punch a guy with a knife and you don't one shot him....you're going to have a bad time....(didn't want people thinking I was insinuating one should always punch their way out of a knife fight).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","user":"CeltSPZ","date":". 08-25-2017, 07:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-26T09:50:41-07:00","id":1134162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Direful Spring</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1133889#post1133889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with a lot of martial art styles is that people practice them in a too controlled environment, then when you get thrown into all the chaos, adrenaline and violence of an actual street fight it all goes to shit. A particularly example of this I often see is the way people suggest you disarm a knife wielder, most of the ways suggested are ridicules and you'll get your fingers sliced off trying, fights happen FAST and they are never clean.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a question of art-oriented vs. sports-oriented vs. lethal combat-oriented training, and is mostly (mostly!) orthogonal to what types of manoeuvres a style teaches.<br /> <br /> Also, when talking about how imba a specialty is, consider that <i>at best</i> it provides an about +10% improvement (reroll about 10% of dice), which is a whopping +1 die-equivalent for someone with a dice pool of ten. 'At best' because you probably still run into less than 100% situations where you can use your spec.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12622,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","user":"vicky_molokh","date":". 08-26-2017, 12:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","name":"vicky_molokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12622_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-26T15:11:27-07:00","id":1134273,"title":"","content":"Questions:<br /> 1)Do you think that adding successes on attack are too much to add on damage rolls?<br /> 2)Would it be better to add only 10ns from attack rolls to damage rolls? 2 successes with a specialty.<br /> <br /> I find it really troubling when you make a called shot and you end up removing half the body from your victim because you had good luck on the attack roll, I say that because I had many bad experiences with reroll 10's in the past and the same problem with 10' double succs.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12906-story-letter","name":"Story Letter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12906_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-26T15:40:34-07:00","id":1134284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Story Letter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1134273#post1134273\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Questions:<br /> 1)Do you think that adding successes on attack are too much to add on damage rolls?<br /> 2)Would it be better to add only 10ns from attack rolls to damage rolls? 2 successes with a specialty.<br /> <br /> I find it really troubling when you make a called shot and you end up removing half the body from your victim because you had good luck on the attack roll, I say that because I had many bad experiences with reroll 10's in the past and the same problem with 10' double succs.</div> </div> </div> </div>Adding successes to attacks does contribute to lethality, and, more disappointingly, to the usefulness of Dex compared to Str (since adding a Dex die not only improves your chance to hit, but also adds half a damage die on average).<br /> Due to the way difficulties interact with success-damage-adding, called shots can sometimes end up being <i>less</i> useful than non-called shots in terms of damage. This seems to be more prominent for the more difficult called shots whose primary purpose is doing more damage, such as head or heart (and I don't mean for the purposes of staking).<br /> It's a bit hard to judge lucky shots because they depend <i>both</i> on unusual difficulties and on unusual dice results, and usually the less common end results tend to be more memorable, making the statistics <i>skewed in our memories</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","name":"vicky_molokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12622_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-27T02:10:39-07:00","id":1134400,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vicky_molokh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1134284#post1134284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Adding successes to attacks does contribute to lethality, and, more disappointingly, to the usefulness of Dex compared to Str (since adding a Dex die not only improves your chance to hit, but also adds half a damage die on average).</div> </div> </div> </div>These are the two options I am thinking regarding combat, <br /> <br /> Dexterity has to do only with accuracy and is important on initiative and splitting dice pools for attacks, yes and dodge too.<br /> Strength has to do only with damage and is important for grappling maneuvers and throwing distances, y<span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">es and lifting/breaking objects.</span></span></span><br /> Stamina has to do only with soaking Damage and is important Regeneration, and checks to ignore pain or resist effects from brutal punishment, yes an holding breath while choking.<br /> <br /> A) No attribute related roll is ever added to another like accuracy successes to damage rolls etc etc<br /> B) There can be added attribute related roll to other rolls, so accuracy rolls are added to damage while attacking, dexterity or strength rolls for blocking maneuvers add successes on soaking rolls, etc etc <br /> <br /> What option you think is the best, taking into cosideration that the 10' rule should play a more important role ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12906-story-letter","name":"Story Letter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12906_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T14:09:35-07:00","id":1378344,"title":"","content":"Um, hello everyone. o/<br /> <br /> Sorry for necroposting but it seems that the topic is the same, so...<br /> I'm playing a wolf theurge and wanted to pick &quot;sense lies&quot; as Empathy specialization, but in the oWoD lying is man+subterfuge on diff int+empathy. Why does the manual lists &quot;sense lies&quot; as a possible specialization if I don't roll for detecting lies?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25122-theroseblack","name":"TheRoseBlack","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T14:43:22-07:00","id":1378362,"title":"","content":"Because the oWoD rules are messy like that after many years of things being written, rules changed, and different developers/authors, etc.<br /> <br /> For example, initiative used to be a Dex + Wits roll, making certain suggested Dex and Wits specialties make sense as they'd apply to that roll. Then initiative was changed to simply adding Dex and Wits to a single d10 roll... but the books never stopped using those specialty suggests even without rolls to use them on.<br /> <br /> Ultimately you either need to:<br /> <br /> 1) Talk to your ST about how strict they are going to be with the Dramatic Systems as examples vs. hard rules. If your ST treats them as examples, you'll probably roll Empathy against lies sometimes depending on how the ST feels like doing things. If the ST treats those as hard rules, you'll want to consider a different specialty that didn't suffer this fate.<br /> <br /> 2) Talk to your ST about a way for specialties to apply to static mechanics (Feats of Strength, speed, Initiative, when your traits are used as difficulties, etc.) so that specialties make sense in these situations.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-04T12:03:29-07:00","id":1378595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1132593-specialization-doubt?p=1378362#post1378362\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because the oWoD rules are messy like that after many years of things being written, rules changed, and different developers/authors, etc.<br /> <br /> For example, initiative used to be a Dex + Wits roll, making certain suggested Dex and Wits specialties make sense as they'd apply to that roll. Then initiative was changed to simply adding Dex and Wits to a single d10 roll... but the books never stopped using those specialty suggests even without rolls to use them on.<br /> <br /> Ultimately you either need to:<br /> <br /> 1) Talk to your ST about how strict they are going to be with the Dramatic Systems as examples vs. hard rules. If your ST treats them as examples, you'll probably roll Empathy against lies sometimes depending on how the ST feels like doing things. If the ST treats those as hard rules, you'll want to consider a different specialty that didn't suffer this fate.<br /> <br /> 2) Talk to your ST about a way for specialties to apply to static mechanics (Feats of Strength, speed, Initiative, when your traits are used as difficulties, etc.) so that specialties make sense in these situations.</div> </div> </div> </div>Slight correction: originally, it was Wits + Alertness as an actual roll and mass comparison of successes, which is echoed in Initiative for 2nd and 3rd editions of Exalted, which use Wits + Awareness as Join Battle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}